# Bliss v1 Memory Model & Pointer Hierarchy

## 1. Design Philosophy — Segregation of Interactions

Bliss is founded on a simple principle:

> **The visual complexity of an operation must scale with its inherent danger.**

Rather than exposing every memory interaction through a single abstraction, Bliss divides memory access into four distinct tiers.

Each tier possesses:

* a dedicated syntax,
* explicit semantic rules,
* clear purpose,
* and a narrowly defined scope.

The objective is not merely memory safety, but **visual honesty**. Safe interactions should remain lightweight, while increasingly dangerous operations should become progressively more explicit.

A second, equally important design rule governs how the tiers relate to each other:

> **Every reinterpretation of memory is an explicit statement. Nothing is ever reinterpreted by a bare assignment.**

Two dedicated statements exist for this: `transform` (Tier 3 → Tier 3) and `view` (Tier 3 → Tier 2). Both are covered in detail below.

---

## 2. Memory Interaction Hierarchy

| Tier       | Syntax                        | Purpose                                  |
| ---------- | ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------- |
| **Tier 1** | `` ` ``, `let`                 | Safe values and temporary references      |
| **Tier 2** | `$Type`, `.$field`, `view`     | Foreign memory references (views)         |
| **Tier 3** | `ptr`, `[ \| ]`, `transform`   | Typed raw pointers                        |
| **Tier 4** | `hdl`, `#[ \| ]`               | Physical hardware handles                 |

`ptr` is the hub of the system. It is the only tier that can produce a Tier 2 view (via `view`) or another Tier 3 pointer of a different type (via `transform`). `let`/`` ` `` and `hdl` never convert to or from anything else — they are dead ends on both sides.

```
let / `            (isolated)

ptr: u8 (malloc) ──transform──▶ ptr: A ──transform──▶ ptr: B ──▶ ...
                 └──view──────▶ $A   (terminal — no further view/transform)

hdl                 (isolated)
```

