## 7. Arrays

Bliss has two kinds of array, distinguished only by whether their size is known at compile time or determined at runtime — the declaration syntax is identical in both cases, matching C's own lack of a syntactic distinction (C's VLAs use the same declaration form as fixed arrays).

```bliss
let arr: i32[10];   // compile-time fixed size

let n = computeSize();
let buf: i32[n];    // runtime-fixed: size is a runtime value, fixed once created
```

An array is a plain value under Tier 1's rules — bound with `let`, and addressable only if `unsafe`.

### Indexing

Arrays reuse the exact offset operator defined for pointers, since both are contiguous, offsettable memory:

```bliss
[arr | 3]              // read
[arr | 3] = 10;        // write
```

### Handing an Array Off as a Pointer

There is no implicit array-to-pointer decay. An array becomes a `ptr` the same way any other value does — `unsafe` plus `adrs`:

```bliss
unsafe let arr: i32[10];

ptr p: i32 = adrs arr;
```

### Strings Are Not Arrays

Bliss's standard library does not represent strings as `[char]`. Instead, it pairs an explicit pointer field with an explicit length, using the ordinary field-bracket rule from §4:

```bliss
data StrData {

    [char] str;   // a pointer to char — Point 2's field-bracket rule, reused verbatim
    u32 len;

}
```

Nothing new is introduced here — `StrData` is simply an ordinary `data` declaration using a pointer field, demonstrating that the field-bracket convention is general-purpose rather than array-specific.

