## 8. Declaration Modifier Summary

| Modifier  | Meaning |
|-----------|---------|
| `unsafe`  | This `let` binding or `data` field is addressable via `adrs`. Does not change its category. |
| `ptr`     | This variable or field holds a typed raw pointer. |
| `$Type`   | This variable or field holds a view. Produced only via `view src as name: Type;`. |
| `hdl`     | This variable is a fixed physical address. Never derived from, or converted to, anything else. |

```bliss
i32 x;                  // plain value, not addressable
unsafe i32 x;           // addressable value
ptr x: i32;             // typed raw pointer
$Node x;                 // view
unsafe ptr x: i32;      // addressable pointer variable itself
```

---

## 9. Summary

| Interaction | Syntax                     | Arithmetic | Can Reinterpret Into        | Storable / Returnable | Addressable by default |
| ----------- | -------------------------- | ---------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------- |
| Value       | `let x`                    | No         | —                              | Yes (storage only)        | No (requires `unsafe`)     |
| Reference   | `` `x ``                   | No         | —                              | No                        | No                         |
| Array       | `let x: T[N]`, `[x \| i]`  | Offset only| `ptr` (via `adrs`)             | Yes (storage only)        | No (requires `unsafe`)     |
| View        | `$Type`, `.$field`         | No         | Terminal — nothing             | Yes                       | No (requires `unsafe`)     |
| Pointer     | `ptr x: T`, `[ \| ]`        | Yes        | `ptr` (`transform`), `$Type` (`view`) | Yes                | No (requires `unsafe`)     |
| Handle      | `hdl`, `#[ \| ]`            | Yes        | Nothing                        | —                         | —                          |

---

## Core Principle

* **References and values** handle almost all application code.
* **Views** give safe, field-based traversal of data structures — reached from a pointer via `view`, and terminal once reached.
* **Pointers** are the hub of the unsafe system — always typed, reinterpretable into one another via `transform`, and the sole origin of views.
* **Handles** are isolated on both sides — hardware addresses that never mix with the managed or pointer world.
* **Arrays** are ordinary values, offsettable like pointers, but only become pointers through the same `unsafe` + `adrs` path any other value would use.

> **The syntax should honestly communicate the level of responsibility expected from the programmer — and every reinterpretation of memory must be a statement you can point to, never a side effect of an assignment.**